  "body": "## What changed\n\n- Ten mill scripts now declare `axisInversion = true`: `mazak.cps`, `doosan-dnm.cps`, `sinumerik.cps`, `heidenhain.cps`, `mach4.cps`, `grblhal.cps`, `tormach.cps`, `tinyg.cps`, `carvera.cps` and `linuxcnc.cps`.\n- Each of them posts a section with a flipped WCS X or Y (#1649).\n- Each of them posts for a machine with an inverted X or Y (#374). The two cases use the same flag.\n- Each script writes the section note `WCS X FLIPPED - PROGRAM MIRRORED` in its comment form. A plain section after a flipped section does not repeat the note.\n- Each script writes the header note `MACHINE X+ MOVES LEFT - INVERTED AXIS`. Doosan DNM and Mach4 had this note already.\n- The refusal hint \"(Fanuc, Haas)\" is now \"(each built-in mill post does)\". This is in `post::invert`, `post::wcs_flip`, `app::setup_wcs_flip` and the `set_wcs_flip` MCP tool description.\n- `linuxcnc.cps` is not in the list of the issue. It is a mill script, and \"Done means\" says \"each mill script\".\n- No script has a recorded reason why it cannot. `docs/decisions/2475-wcs-flip-mill-posts.md` has the table for each dialect.\n\n## Cause\n\nOnly `fanuc.cps` and `haas.cps` declared `axisInversion = true`. The engine refuses the mirror for a script without the flag. No test had read the mirrored program of the other dialects.\n\n## Tests (pins, mutation results)\n\nNew file `crates/post/tests/wcs_flip_dialects.rs`, with the new reader `crates/post/tests/mirror_trace/mod.rs`.\n\nThe reader reads a posted program as a controller whose X, Y, or X and Y run reversed. It gives the physical cut runs as dense polylines. The test compares each run with the CAM path at equal fractions of the run length. It compares the physical compensation side. It checks that no rapid ends over an X Y that the CAM path does not visit. The reader stops on a G word that it does not model.\n\nThe reader reads these forms:\n- I/J/K arcs in G17, G18 and G19, and a helix in the dialects that support it\n- Heidenhain planar Cartesian arcs; the reader rejects a Cartesian helix\n- R-word arcs (`useRadiusArcs`)\n- TinyG half-turn arcs, and the chords of TinyG and Heidenhain\n- the Heidenhain `I J` centre block\n- G81, the Heidenhain G200 with M99, and a hole as plain moves\n\nFanuc and Haas are in the same table. They calibrate the reader against the scripts that #374 and #1649 verified.\n\nPins:\n- `a_flipped_wcs_cuts_the_cam_path_in_each_mill_dialect` (12 scripts, flip X, Y, XY, with the converse)\n- `an_inverted_machine_axis_cuts_the_cam_path_in_each_mill_dialect` (12 scripts, inverted X, Y, XY, with the converse)\n- `radius_word_arcs_follow_the_mirror` (the 7 scripts with `useRadiusArcs`)\n- `each_dialect_names_the_flipped_axes_and_the_inverted_axes`\n- `a_plain_section_after_a_flipped_one_writes_no_flip_note`\n- `probing_on_an_inverted_axis_stays_refused_in_the_new_dialects`\n- `flipped_x_goldens_for_the_new_dialects`\n- `heidenhain_refuses_cartesian_helices_by_operation_name`\n- `heidenhain_reader_rejects_cartesian_helices`\n\nThe converse is in the two trace tests. The standard program, read by the reversed controller, must stray. This holds for each script and each case.\n\nMutations:\n- The ten scripts as on `upstream/main`: the 7 pins are red. The trace tests stop with \"must post: ... does not declare axisInversion = true\". Restored: the 7 pins are green.\n- `mach4.cps` without the `wcsFlip = \"\"` reset at the section end: `a_plain_section_after_a_flipped_one_writes_no_flip_note` is red. Restored: green.\n\nReview-fix mutations:\n- Removed the Heidenhain helix refusal: `heidenhain_refuses_cartesian_helices_by_operation_name` is red. Restored: green.\n- Removed the reader guard: `heidenhain_reader_rejects_cartesian_helices` is red. Restored: green.\n- Both guards restored: all 9 dialect tests are green.\n\nChanged tests (the lists that this change extends):\n- `wcs_flip.rs` `a_script_that_is_not_verified_for_a_mirror_refuses_by_name`: it now uses the two lathe scripts and `mazak.cps` with the flag line removed.\n- `inverted_post.rs` `every_unverified_dialect_refuses_by_name`: the same, with `sinumerik.cps`, and the new hint text.\n- `crates/app/tests/wcs_flip_post.rs` `a_flipped_and_rotated_wcs_and_an_unverified_post_refuse_by_name`: it selects Fanuc Lathe, because Mazak carries the flip now.\n\nGate on the review fix: `cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main` is green: 150 steps, 0 failed. Post tests: 785 passed. App tests: 736 passed, 1 ignored. `cargo clippy -j 6 -p camcraft-post -p camcraft-app --all-targets -- -D warnings` passed. The full gate ran on the final unchanged tree after the recorded refusal fixtures were updated.\n\n## Goldens moved\n\nTen goldens are new. The Heidenhain golden uses the planar fixture after the review fix: `crates/post/tests/golden/wcs_flip/<script>_x.nc`, the X-flipped program of each new dialect.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- A machine. No program ran on a control.\n- Tapping, peck and bore cycles on the mirrored stream. They take the same mirrored X Y as G81. Their other words have no X or Y meaning.\n- The Heidenhain G205, G206 and G207 calls.\n- Probing. It stays refused: in a flipped WCS by the engine, and on an inverted machine axis because the ten scripts do not declare `probeAxisInversion`.\n- A flipped Z, a flipped and rotated WCS, tilted moves. The engine refuses them as before.\n- The Mach4 trunnion (3+2) path. The engine refuses a tilted move under a mirror.\n- The line \"Fanuc and Haas are\" in `.claude/skills/camcraft-mcp/SKILL.md`. The worker session could not write to `.claude/skills`. The line is stale after this PR.\n\nReview fix: the Heidenhain post refuses a native XY helix by name, with or without a flipped WCS. The reader rejects Cartesian G02/G03 blocks that change Z. The public TNC 640 ISO manual, ID 892909-25, pages 271-272, specifies a polar helix. Planar XY arcs and the existing XZ/YZ chord paths keep their trace proof.\n\n## Overlaps\n\n- #2698 changes `assets/posts/AGENTS.md` and `sodick-wire.cps`. It touches the same file, a different paragraph. Whichever merges second refreshes.\n- No open PR changes the ten scripts or the flip tests.\n\nEffort: high\n\nChangelog: The Mazak, Doosan DNM, Siemens 840D, Heidenhain, Mach4, grblHAL, Tormach PathPilot, TinyG, Carvera and LinuxCNC posts post a flipped WCS X or Y and a machine with an inverted X or Y as a mirrored program, with a note that names the axes (#2475)\n\nFixes #2475\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n",
